{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Limit datauser to 100 MB of storage under /srv/data using filesystem-level enforcement, with the configured state remaining operational.","status":"satisfied","summary":"datauser owns /srv/data, an ext4 user-quota hard limit of 102400 KiB is active, and a 110 MiB write by that account was rejected with “Quota exceeded.” After restart, the changed boot identity, active quota mount, preserved limit, marker checksum, and another rejected oversized write directly demonstrate persistence and enforcement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-74d85e81deea4751","cmd-50b17f62720b4674","cmd-e7a1bfdf51694182","cmd-92bc2c0130bc4975","cmd-30d0485c440d434a","cmd-bc14808a916a47e6","cmd-a697cc7c92384e26","cmd-6381725f128b490c","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The before and after lifecycle observations both report Alpine Linux 3.23.3 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts; post-restart command evidence agrees.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","cmd-bc14808a916a47e6"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"quota-tools 4.10-r0 was installed from the configured Alpine 3.23 repositories and successfully provided working ext4 quota enforcement without changing the OS release or kernel.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b3ef1c4f173d4118","cmd-b3a70b6c495644b8","cmd-6381725f128b490c","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"Implementation mutations were relevant and bounded: package installation, account/path creation, quota configuration, enforcement tests, restart, and cleanup all supported the requested outcome or mandatory restart evidence. Two minor retained residues reduce hygiene: cmd-b3ef1c4f173d4118 created /tmp/apk-update.log, which remained in the final lifecycle snapshot, and cmd-dca5cd60f60548b5 created /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota with no later cleanup shown. Both were low-risk; the quota-test files and output files were removed. No new network exposure or additional package-health damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b3ef1c4f173d4118","cmd-dca5cd60f60548b5","cmd-6381725f128b490c","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Filesystem quota enforcement was directly tested both before and after a fully evidenced node restart, while the operating-system release and kernel remained unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot observations.","limitations":["The user quota applies to all datauser-owned blocks on the root filesystem, not solely to /srv/data. This still enforces the requested ceiling under /srv/data, but its scope is broader.","Alpine lacks the systemd failed-unit probe used by the lifecycle collector, so that probe could not independently assess failed services; direct OpenRC and quota checks showed the relevant recovered state.","No final command explicitly inventoried or removed /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota; its retention is inferred from its creation and the complete timeline showing no deletion."]}
